{
  "id": "COMPOUND:847 - DISEASE:1858",
  "verbalization": "Tacrolimus, a cyclosporine-like immunosuppressant, appears to exert its effects through multiple indirect pathways that ultimately lead to the development of Liposarcoma, Myxoid. The evidence suggests that Tacrolimus influences a gene called testis-specific transcript, Y-linked 11 (non-protein coding), which in turn causes Liposarcoma, Myxoid. Similarly, Tacrolimus affects T cell receptor beta variable 30 (gene/pseudogene), which is encoded by Mastermind-like protein 3 (Mam-3), and this gene product also contributes to the condition. Additionally, Tacrolimus impacts castor zinc finger 1, which is encoded by ATP-dependent RNA helicase DDX3X, and this gene product also leads to Liposarcoma, Myxoid. These pathways collectively highlight a mechanism by which Tacrolimus may induce the disease through the modulation of gene expression and cellular processes. The integration of these diverse molecular interactions underscores a potential role for Tacrolimus in the pathogenesis of Liposarcoma, Myxoid, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated.",
